# /etc/ssh/sshd_config — Novi Linux (RFC 0022)
#
# Novi's own, not the sshd_config upstream generates. Upstream's
# defaults are written for a system whose root account has a password;
# the shipped Novi root account has an EMPTY password field, so a few
# of them have to be different here and the differences are the reason
# this file exists rather than being taken from the build tree.
#
# Everything not mentioned is OpenSSH's default, on purpose. A config
# that restates defaults is a config that silently freezes them at
# whatever they were the day it was written.

# ── Who may log in ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Root may not, over ssh, at all -- not even with a key. /etc/shadow
# ships with `root::` (no password), which is deliberate for an
# installable live image and catastrophic the moment a port is open;
# `prohibit-password` would still be one `passwd` away from being the
# account everyone on the internet tries first. Root logs in at the
# console, or someone in `wheel` uses `su`.
PermitRootLogin no

# Password authentication is ON, and that is safe here for a reason
# worth stating rather than assuming, because the reason is not this
# line -- it is what sits behind it.
#
# A FRESH NOVI HAS NOBODY WHO CAN LOG IN. Not "nobody with a good
# password": nobody. root has an empty hash and is refused by
# PermitRootLogin above and by PermitEmptyPasswords below. Every other
# account novi-state or novi-install creates starts LOCKED (`!` in
# /etc/shadow, RFC 0005), and OpenSSH refuses a locked account
# outright -- with a key, too, which is easy to assume it does not.
# Verified: an account with an authorized_keys it owns and a `!` in
# shadow gets "Permission denied (publickey,password)".
#
# So opening port 22 on a machine nobody has touched exposes nothing
# that can be authenticated to, and the act that changes that is
# somebody at the console running `passwd`. Turning password auth off
# would be marginally stricter and would also mean nobody can ever
# reach a machine they did not pre-seed a key onto -- not a trade a
# general-purpose OS gets to make on its users' behalf.
PasswordAuthentication yes
PermitEmptyPasswords no
PubkeyAuthentication yes

# There is deliberately no `UsePAM no` here. There is no PAM on this
# system -- OpenSSH is built --without-pam -- and a build without it
# does not merely default the option off, it does not KNOW the keyword:
# sshd prints "line 39: Unsupported option UsePAM" on every start and
# on every `sshd -t`. A line whose only effect is a warning about
# itself is worse than the absence it was documenting.

# ── What runs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# scp has spoken SFTP rather than the old rcp protocol since OpenSSH
# 9.0, so this line is what makes `scp` to this machine work at all,
# not just `sftp`. The path is where the openssh-server package puts
# it, which is where configure baked it in.
Subsystem sftp /usr/libexec/sftp-server

# ── Logging ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The service runs `sshd -D -e`, so this goes to stderr and into s6's
# catch-all log with everything else. VERBOSE records the fingerprint
# of the key each session authenticated with, which is the one line
# anybody ever wants after the fact.
LogLevel VERBOSE
